!/usr/bin/env bash
---
id: zdots-ruby-bump
title: "Zdots Ruby Latest-Stable Bumper"
purpose: Keep the pinned Ruby at the latest STABLE release and rebuild its gem world.
rationale: The repo deliberately pins Ruby (mise.toml + etc/ruby-version + global
           mise config) for CI/bootstrap reproducibility. "Always run latest Ruby"
           is therefore implemented as an automated bump of those pins — not a
           floating spec — so CI still sees a concrete version.
---
bin/zdots-ruby-bump — Resolve latest stable Ruby, rewrite the three pin sites in
lockstep, then reconcile that Ruby's gem world: default-gems + the project bundle.

Why the bundle step matters: a Ruby version bump gives the new version an empty
bundle. Without `bundle install`, every Bundler-backed command (zdots-ctx migrate,
sync-history) dies with Bundler::GemNotFound. This script closes that gap so a bump
is never left half-done.

Usage:
  zdots-ruby-bump            # bump to latest stable if newer, then reconcile gems
  zdots-ruby-bump --check    # report current vs latest, exit 0 if current, 10 if behind
  zdots-ruby-bump --dry-run  # show what would change, make no changes

Exit codes: 0 = up to date / bumped OK · 10 = (--check only) a newer stable exists
            1 = error
Latest STABLE = highest X.Y.Z with no pre-release suffix. sort -V for correct
semantic ordering (4.0.10 > 4.0.5); never trust lexical sort here.
Current pin as recorded in the repo mise.toml (the source of truth for CI).
--- Reconcile the gem world for the (now-pinned) Ruby --------------------------
Default (global) gems — the list that sbin/zdots-brain requires at runtime.
Project bundle — required by every Bundler-backed brain command. This is the
step whose absence leaves `zdots-ctx migrate` dead after a Ruby bump.
.fi
